# PULSEY

[![PyPI version](https://img.shields.io/pypi/v/PULSEY.svg)](https://pypi.org/project/PULSEY/)
[![Documentation Status](https://readthedocs.org/projects/pulsey/badge/?version=latest)](https://pulsey.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)
[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.x-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)

PULSEY is a Python package for simulating stellar pulsations with
time-dependent spherical harmonic surface maps. It builds on
[`jaxoplanet.starry`](https://jax.exoplanet.codes/) to combine pulsation modes,
compute disk-integrated flux curves, render evolving stellar surfaces, and
sample local surface fluxes on longitude-latitude grids.

The package is designed around a compact workflow:

1. Define one or more pulsation modes with `(l, m)` spherical harmonic indices.
2. Assign frequencies, amplitudes, and phases to those modes.
3. Evaluate the evolving map or light curve over time.
4. Inspect the surface at specific map coordinates with `discretizeSurface`.

## Features

- Combine multiple spherical harmonic pulsation modes into one evolving stellar
  surface.
- Compute dense STARRY-compatible map coefficient vectors with `computeMap`.
- Compute disk-integrated flux curves with `computeFlux`.
- Insert the pulsating star into a simple eclipsing binary model.
- Render and animate stellar surface maps.
- Discretize the surface and sample local flux/intensity values at specific
  longitude and latitude points.
- Build Sphinx documentation for Read the Docs.

## Installation

Install the latest released package from PyPI:

```bash
pip install PULSEY
```

Install from a local checkout while developing:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/andrew-ayala/PULSEY.git
cd PULSEY
pip install -e .
```

PULSEY currently depends on `numpy`, `jax`, and `jaxoplanet`.

## Quick Start

```python
import numpy as np
import PULSEY as p

time = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 200)

pulse = p.star(
    lmModes=[[1, 0], [2, 1]],
    freq=[1.0, 2.5],
    amp=[0.01, 0.005],
    phase=[0.0, 0.25],
    inc=90.0,
)

flux = pulse.computeFlux(time)
```

`computeFlux` returns the disk-integrated flux at each requested time.

## Surface Sampling

Use `discretizeSurface` to evaluate local surface fluxes at longitude and
latitude points. This samples the map itself, not the disk-integrated light
curve.

```python
import numpy as np
import PULSEY as p

pulse = p.star(
    lmModes=[[1, 0]],
    freq=1.0,
    amp=0.1,
    phase=0.0,
    inc=90.0,
)

lon, lat, surface_flux = pulse.discretizeSurface(
    time=0.25,
    lon=[0.0, 90.0, 180.0],
    lat=[0.0, 45.0, -30.0],
    grid=False,
)

print(np.asarray(surface_flux))
```

With `grid=False`, coordinates are paired point-by-point:

```text
(lon=0,   lat=0)    -> sampled flux
(lon=90,  lat=45)   -> sampled flux
(lon=180, lat=-30)  -> sampled flux
```

Generate a full latitude-longitude grid instead:

```python
lon, lat, surface_flux = pulse.discretizeSurface(
    time=0.0,
    nLon=72,
    nLat=36,
)
```

When multiple times are supplied, the returned flux array prepends a time axis
to the coordinate shape.

## Mollweide Surface Plot

```python
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

lon, lat, surface_flux = pulse.discretizeSurface(
    time=0.25,
    nLon=181,
    nLat=91,
)

fig = plt.figure(figsize=(9, 4.8))
ax = fig.add_subplot(111, projection="mollweide")

mesh = ax.pcolormesh(
    np.deg2rad(np.asarray(lon)),
    np.deg2rad(np.asarray(lat)),
    np.asarray(surface_flux),
    cmap="seismic_r",
    shading="auto",
)

ax.grid(True, alpha=0.35)
ax.set_title("PULSEY surface flux at t = 0.25")

cbar = fig.colorbar(mesh, ax=ax, orientation="horizontal", pad=0.08)
cbar.set_label("local surface flux")

plt.show()
```

## Documentation

The documentation source lives in [`docs/`](docs/) and is configured for Read
the Docs with [`.readthedocs.yaml`](.readthedocs.yaml).

- Read the Docs: <https://pulsey.readthedocs.io/en/latest/>
- API reference: <https://pulsey.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html>
- Usage guide: <https://pulsey.readthedocs.io/en/latest/usage.html>

Build the documentation locally:

```bash
pip install -r docs/requirements.txt
sphinx-build -b html docs docs/_build/html
```

Then open:

```text
docs/_build/html/index.html
```

## Development

Build a wheel locally:

```bash
python -m pip wheel --no-deps --wheel-dir dist .
```

Run the test suite:

```bash
python -m pytest
```

## Project Layout

```text
PULSEY/
├── PULSEY/              # Package source
│   ├── PULSEY.py        # Pulsation model and surface sampling tools
│   └── __init__.py
├── docs/                # Sphinx and Read the Docs documentation
├── examples/            # Example notebooks and rendered animations
├── tests/               # Test suite
├── pyproject.toml       # Package metadata and build configuration
└── README.md
```

